      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "cb2e2e55-8936-54d7-a33c-03469d398524",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} z - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},3 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2 \\pi) & =⟨3,0,6 \\pi⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(3,0,0)$ and $(3,0,6 \\pi).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2 \\pi))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,0,6 \\pi)-f(3,0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(6 \\pi\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\left(54 \\pi+0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- 108 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "fa67956c-515d-580e-8ab9-dbe6238b9841",
