        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} - 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 2,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},4,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2,1)$ and $(e^{2},4,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},4,e^{-2})-f(1,2,1) \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-12 + 48 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =-18 + 48 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
